  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The game was decided in the second inning.",
    "what_this_means": "Milwaukee scored four runs before anyone warmed up in the bullpen. Max Fried kept them contained after that, but by then the Yankees were chasing a deficit they couldn't close. Jose Caballero went 2-for-3 and did what he could. Judge went 1-for-4. The rest of the lineup logged fourteen strikeouts and three hits. You don't win shutouts like that. The math doesn't work when one inning costs you the whole game and the bats can't find anything to hit. They never led. They never even tied it again after the first. That's the shape of a blowout. Clean and early and impossible to recover from.",
    "what_to_watch": "The fragile feeling that came with yesterday's loss just got louder. Four in a row now without scoring more than three runs. The ERA climbed from 3.04 to 3.12 not because of a bad outing but because good pitching is worthless when the offense can't back it up. Fried was fine. The problem is the fourteen strikeouts and the complete absence of anything in the middle innings. When Judge is your cleanup hitter and he goes 1-for-4, and no one else steps in, you're not building anything. You're hoping something breaks. They had the chance to stay within reach after the second and they didn't take it. That's the cost of this kind of consistency.",
